{
  "teamId": 136,
  "year": 2026,
  "narrative": "The 2026 Seattle Mariners opened the season with a jolt of electricity, as rookie Cole Young announced himself to the baseball world with a pair of early home runs and timely hits that had T-Mobile Park buzzing with genuine optimism. Young's three-run blast on Opening Day, driving in Arozarena and Canzone, set an encouraging tone, and the next afternoon Julio Rodríguez delivered a clutch single to cap off back-to-back promising performances. Through early April, the Mariners looked like a team with a pulse — young talent stepping up, veterans contributing, and the bullpen holding leads.\n\nThe cracks, however, began to show in mid-April and deepened through the spring. The bullpen — anchored by a struggling Andrés Muñoz — became the team's most glaring wound. Muñoz, once the most electric closer in the American League, repeatedly squandered late-game leads, surrendering key hits to Jac Caglianone in May and Kevin McGonigle in early June in moments that shifted wins into losses. Relief pitcher José A. Ferrer also had damaging outings, with a brutal stretch in June that saw the Mariners drop winnable games against opponents they needed to beat. The run differential told the story: Seattle simply couldn't protect leads.\n\nThe summer months offered flashes of what this team could be. Cole Young's 12th homer in July, Josh Naylor's grand slam on June 8th, and Dominic Canzone's three-run blast on July 26th gave fans reasons to believe in a potential rally. Randy Arozarena delivered the most dramatic moment of the entire season on August 22nd — a two-run walk-off home run worth nearly a full win of probability — a reminder that this roster had legitimate star power. But for every Arozarena moonshot, Muñoz gave one back, and the team never found the consistency needed to string together winning stretches in a competitive AL Wild Card race.\n\nBy September, the Mariners' fate was sealed. Muñoz surrendered a two-run homer to Lawrence Butler in mid-September that effectively closed the door on any remaining playoff hopes, and Eduard Bazardo's late-season implosions added salt to the wound. Seattle finished 76-86, a team that showed glimpses of an exciting young core — Young, Rodríguez, Canzone — but was dragged down by bullpen fragility and an inability to win close games. The 2026 season will be remembered as a painful inflection point: the kids are coming, but the bridge generation couldn't hold the fort.",
